        "curated_prompt": "Table.for_portion method incorrectly validates and assigns attributes\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `for_portion` method on Table objects has incorrect logic that prevents it from working properly. When trying to use `for_portion` with temporal queries, it raises an AttributeError even when it should be allowed.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom pypika import Query, Table, SYSTEM_TIME\n\ntable_abc = Table('abc')\n\n# This should work but raises AttributeError\nq = Query.from_(table_abc.for_portion(SYSTEM_TIME.from_to('2020-01-01', '2020-02-01'))).delete()\nprint(str(q))\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```\nDELETE FROM \"abc\" FOR PORTION OF SYSTEM_TIME FROM '2020-01-01' TO '2020-02-01'\n```\n\nActual output:\n```\nAttributeError: 'Query' object already has attribute for_portion\n```\n\nThe same issue occurs with update queries:\n\n```python\nq = Query.update(table_abc.for_portion(SYSTEM_TIME.from_to('2020-01-01', '2020-02-01'))).set('foo', 'bar')\nprint(str(q))\n```\n\nThis should produce:\n```\nUPDATE \"abc\" FOR PORTION OF SYSTEM_TIME FROM '2020-01-01' TO '2020-02-01' SET \"foo\"='bar'\n```\n\nBut instead raises the same AttributeError.",
        "reviewed_by": "Codex source-bound original API import and actual before/after SQL review 2026-10-28",
        "reason": "SYSTEM_TIME is a public instance exported by pypika.__init__, not pypika.terms. The original import fails even after the production repair. Only that import is corrected; both specified SQL outputs and the repair/original oracle are unchanged.",
